  "sections": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "content": "Question words are your most powerful tool as a beginner. Even with limited vocabulary, you can point at things, ask what they're called, ask where things are, and ask someone to slow down. Master these and you can navigate almost any situation."
    },
    {
      "type": "examples",
      "items": [
        {
          "catalan": "qui?",
          "english": "who?",
          "note": "kee"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "què?",
          "english": "what?",
          "note": "keh"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "on?",
          "english": "where?",
          "note": "on"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "quan?",
          "english": "when?",
          "note": "kwan"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "com?",
          "english": "how?",
          "note": "kom"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "per què?",
          "english": "why?",
          "note": "puhr KEH - two words; perquè (one word) means 'because'"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "quant / quanta?",
          "english": "how much?",
          "note": "kwan / KWAN-tuh - the t in quant is silent"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "quants / quantes?",
          "english": "how many?",
          "note": "kwans / KWAN-tuhs"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "quin / quina?",
          "english": "which? / what?",
          "note": "keen / KEE-nuh"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "rule",
      "content": "To form a question in Catalan, simply put the question word at the start. The verb order is usually: question word + verb + subject. For example: On és la cuina? (Where is the kitchen?) - on (where) + és (is) + la cuina (the kitchen)."
    },
    {
      "type": "examples",
      "items": [
        {
          "catalan": "On és el bany?",
          "english": "Where is the bathroom?",
          "note": "on + és + el bany"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "Qui és?",
          "english": "Who is it?",
          "note": "qui + és"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "Quan dinem?",
          "english": "When do we have lunch?",
          "note": "quan + dinem"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "Com es diu això?",
          "english": "What is this called?",
          "note": "literally 'how does this call itself'"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "Quant val?",
          "english": "How much does it cost?",
          "note": ""
        },
        {
          "catalan": "Quin és el teu nom?",
          "english": "What is your name?",
          "note": "literally 'which is your name'"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "rule",
      "content": "Quant (how much / how many) and quin (which / what) agree with the noun that follows, like an adjective: quant (masculine), quanta (feminine), quants (masculine plural), quantes (feminine plural). In the same way: quin, quina, quins, quines."
    },
    {
      "type": "examples",
      "items": [
        {
          "catalan": "Quants anys tens?",
          "english": "How old are you?",
          "note": "els anys - masculine plural"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "Quantes persones?",
          "english": "How many people?",
          "note": "les persones - feminine plural"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "Quin dia és avui?",
          "english": "What day is it today?",
          "note": "el dia - masculine"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "Quina hora és?",
          "english": "What time is it?",
          "note": "l'hora - feminine"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "rule",
      "content": "A yes/no question has no question word and no extra word like English 'do'. Use the same words as the statement and let your voice rise at the end: Parles anglès. (You speak English.) → Parles anglès? (Do you speak English?)"
    },
    {
      "type": "examples",
      "items": [
        {
          "catalan": "Parles anglès?",
          "english": "Do you speak English?",
          "note": "no word for 'do'"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "Ets de Barcelona?",
          "english": "Are you from Barcelona?",
          "note": ""
        },
        {
          "catalan": "Tens temps?",
          "english": "Do you have time?",
          "note": ""
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "rule",
      "content": "Use 'puc' (can I) and 'pots' (can you) to make polite requests. These come from the verb 'poder' (to be able to). Puc fer servir el bany? (Can I use the bathroom?) - puc + fer servir + el bany. Pots repetir? (Can you repeat?) - pots + repetir."
    },
    {
      "type": "examples",
      "items": [
        {
          "catalan": "Puc fer servir el bany?",
          "english": "Can I use the bathroom?",
          "note": "puc = can I; fer servir = to use"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "Puc ajudar?",
          "english": "Can I help?",
          "note": ""
        },
        {
          "catalan": "Pots repetir?",
          "english": "Can you repeat?",
          "note": "pots = can you"
        },
        {
          "catalan": "Pots parlar més a poc a poc?",
          "english": "Can you speak more slowly?",
          "note": ""
        },
        {
          "catalan": "Pots parlar més fort?",
          "english": "Can you speak louder?",
          "note": ""
        },
        {
          "catalan": "Què vol dir...?",
          "english": "What does ... mean?",
          "note": "vol dir = means / wants to say"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
